BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 677.0 Tm (Microsoft developed exFAT in the 2000s as a successor to FAT32 for larger files and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 660.0 Tm (flash-based removable storage. It appeared in Windows Embedded CE 6.0-era systems and) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 643.0 Tm (spread to consumer removable disks, SDXC cards and desktop operating systems. Microsoft) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 626.0 Tm (later published the exFAT File System Specification, and the format gained broad) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 609.0 Tm (cross-platform implementation support.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 580.0 Tm (The central design goals are explicit in Microsoft's own specification: preserve much of) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 563.0 Tm (FAT's implementation simplicity, support much larger files and devices, and permit) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 546.0 Tm (extensible metadata. exFAT is not FAT32 with a larger file-size field. Its directory entry) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.2 Tf 0.09 0.16 0.24 rg 1 0 0 1 51 529.0 Tm (sets, allocation bitmap and upcase-table semantics require a distinct parser and writer.) Tj ET
